  "title": "Why the Army Blew Up Nearly 5 Tons of Explosives in Iowa",
  "deck": "The Army said the drums were too dangerous to move. EPA monitors detected no airborne lead in the first hour after the blast.",
  "category": "politics",
  "categoryLabel": "U.S. News",
  "type": "article",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-25T21:00:00-05:00",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-25T21:00:00-05:00",
  "body": [
    "By the time the Army decided to blow up more than 9,700 pounds of lead azide at its ammunition plant near Middletown, Iowa, the material had become so unstable that crews couldn’t safely move the drums or open their lids. On Wednesday morning, the Army set off a controlled explosion where the drums were stored.",
    "Lead azide is a compound made of lead and nitrogen. Small amounts are used in detonators to set off larger charges. In Iowa, the Army had 68 deteriorating drums. Crystals had formed on the outside of the containers, including on the lids. The Army said handling them could trigger an accidental explosion.",
    "## What happened to the lead?",
    "The blast broke down the explosive compound. The lead was still there, and officials were concerned that some of it could escape as fine dust. Crews prepared the site to contain that dust, and authorities told residents within 20 miles to stay indoors during the operation. Missouri health officials said before the blast that any lead reaching their state was expected to be at low levels.",
    "EPA air monitors ran throughout the blast. An hour later, the agency said none had detected airborne lead. By late afternoon, an alert told residents they could resume normal activities. EPA said it would keep testing air, soil and water in the area.",
    "The Army says the drums deteriorated rapidly over eight months. Its public account leaves a question about that timeline: When did inspectors first see signs of trouble, and what could the Army still do with the drums at that point?"
  ],
